(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\nprint(f\\\"v1 vs v2 max diff: {torch.max(torch.abs(out1 - out2)):.2e}\\\")\n\nprofile(\\\"v1_current\\\", lambda: v1_current(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta))\nprofile(\\\"v2_stacked_gram\\\", lambda: v2_stacked_gram(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta))\n\n# Also test with alpha=1.0\nprint(\\\"\\\\nalpha=1.0 case:\\\")\nprofile(\\\"v1_current alpha=1\\\", lambda: v1_current(x, wq, wk, wv, 1.0, beta))\nprofile(\\\"v2_stacked_gram alpha=1\\\", lambda: v2_stacked_gram(x, wq, wk, wv, 1.0, beta))\n\nPYEOF\npython3 /tmp/profile_test4.py\"","aggregated_output":"=== Gram multiply variants ===\n  4D broadcast p@G: 6.077 ms\n  reshaped (B,HL,L) @ (B,L,L): 4.939 ms\n  bmm (B*H,L,L) @ (B*H,L,L): 5.654 ms\n  stacked (p1+p_mix) @ G: 9.937 ms\n  reshaped p_mix @ G: 5.026 ms\n  2x reshaped @ G: 10.015 ms\n\n=== Attention output variants ===\n  4D p_mix @ v: 1.326 ms\n  reshaped (B,HL,L) @ (B,HL,D) - no, wrong dims: 0.000 ms\n\n=== Full function comparison ===\nv1 vs v2 max diff: 0.00e+00\n  v1_current: 49.612 ms\n  v2_stacked_gram: 49.251 ms\n\nalpha=1.0 case:\n  v1_current alpha=1: 28.608 ms\n  v2_stacked_gram alpha=1: 28.949 ms\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"agent_message","text":"Interesting findings:\n- Reshaped `(B, HL, L) @ (B, L, L)` is ~18% faster than 4D broadcast for p@G\n- Stacked version is about the same as 2 separate reshaped ones (no benefit from stacking)\n- Overall the full function is only ~0.7% faster with stacked gram\n- Removing `.contiguous()` on q,k,v didn't help much\n\nLet me think about bigger wins. The main costs are projections (19ms) and softmax (11ms) and Gram (10ms).\n\nWait, I just realized something. The reshaped Gram multiply is significantly faster (5.0ms vs 6.1ms = 18% faster). Let me apply that to both p1@G and p_mix@G. That should save about 2ms on the largest case, which is ~4%.\n\nLet me also think about softmax optimization. Softmax on (BHL, L) = (5120, 256) takes ~5.5ms. That's memory bandwidth limited.\n\nActually, wait - let me check something. The softmax of the 4D tensor directly: is it the same speed as view+2D+view? From earlier tests they were about the same. But let me think about whether there's a way to avoid one softmax...\n\nHmm, what if we only compute softmax once? No, p0 and p1 have different logits, so they need different softmaxes.\n\nWhat about computing p1_logits more efficiently? It's only ~1ms, not worth optimizing further.\n\nLet me think about the projections again. 3 matmuls of (B*L, C) @ (C, H*D). For the largest case: (256, 1280) @ (1280, 1280). That's a reasonable-sized GEMM. Can we make it faster?\n\nIdea: use `torch.addmm` or `torch.mm` directly instead of `@`. Unlikely to help, `@` already calls the same BLAS.\n\nIdea: transpose wq, wk, wv once at the start so we don't reshape+transpose each time? No, the reshape is just a view.\n\nActually, let me look at this from a different angle. What if we compute the logits differently to save memory?\n\nlogits = q @ k.T where q and k are both (B, H, L, D). The result is (B, H, L, L).\n\nFLOPs: B * H * (L*D*L) = B*H*L^2*D\n\nAlternative: compute logits directly from x, wq, wk:\nA[h,i,j] = sum_d sum_c x[i,c] * wq[h,d,c] * wk[h,d,c] * x[j,c] / sqrt(D)\nWait that's not right. Let me think again.\n\nq[h,i,d] = sum_c x[i,c] * wq[h,d,c]\nk[h,j,d] = sum_c x[j,c] * wk[h,d,c]\nA[h,i,j] = sum_d q[h,i,d] * k[h,j,d] / sqrt(D)\n= sum_d sum_c1 sum_c2 x[i,c1] * wq[h,d,c1] * x[j,c2] * wk[h,d,c2] / sqrt(D)\n\nThat's way more FLOPs (L^2 * D * C^2). Bad.\n\nOK, the q and k approach is optimal for computing attention logits.\n\nLet me think about another angle: can we compute the output differently?\n\noutput = r * (p_mix @ v)\nwhere v = x @ wv^T\n\nSo output = r * p_mix @ x @ wv^T\n\nThe p_mix @ x part is the expensive one if done directly, but we're using the trick of computing v = x @ wv^T first (which is one of the projections), then computing p_mix @ v (which is B*H*L^2*D FLOPs).\n\nWait, let me reconsider the whole computation:\n\nCost breakdown (for largest case):\n- q proj: 2*L*C*H*D = 2*256*1280*1280 = 838M  (6.4ms)\n- k proj: same = 838M (6.4ms)\n- v proj: same = 838M (6.5ms)\n- logits: 2*H*L^2*D = 2*20*256^2*64 = 167M (1.3ms)\n- softmax x2: ~50M ops equivalent (11ms - memory bound)\n- G: 2*L^2*C = 2*256^2*1280 = 168M (1.5ms)\n- p1@G: 2*H*L^3 = 2*20*256^3 = 671M (5.5ms)\n- p_mix@G: 2*H*L^3 = 671M (5.5ms)\n- p_mix@v: 2*H*L^2*D = 167M (1.3ms)\n- norms + ratio: negligible\n\nThe projections are the biggest cost at ~19ms. Can we reduce this?\n\nWait - what if we don't need all three projections? We definitely need q and k for logits. We need v for output. But what if...\n\nActually, let me think about v differently. v = x @ wv^T is (B, L, H*D) → reshaped to (B, H, L, D).\n\nWhat if we compute p_mix @ v as the output? That's what we do.\n\nIs there a way to avoid computing v explicitly? For instance, compute (p_mix @ x) @ wv^T? That would be p_mix @ x (B*H*L^2*C FLOPs) then @ wv^T (B*H*L*C*D FLOPs). Total: B*H*L^2*C + B*H*L*C*D.\n\nFor the largest case: 20*256^2*1280 + 20*256*1280*64 = 1.68B + 419M = 2.1B FLOPs, vs current approach: v-proj (838M) + p_mix@v (167M) = 1B FLOPs.\n\nCurrent approach is much better (2x fewer FLOPs).\n\nOK so projections are fundamental. Let me see if there's any other angle.\n\nWhat about the softmax? Can we do anything clever?\n\nActually, let me think about this: do we really need p0 explicitly? p_mix = (1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1. And we need p_mix for the output and for normm. We need p1 for norm1.\n\nWhat if we compute normm differently?\n\nnormm = ||m|| = ||u0 + beta*(u1 - u0)|| = ||(1-beta)*u0 + beta*u1||\n\nWe can compute:\nnormm^2 = (1-beta)^2 * norm0^2 + beta^2 * norm1^2 + 2*beta*(1-beta) * (u0 · u1)\n\nWhere u0 · u1 = (p0 @ x) · (p1 @ x) = sum over tokens of (p0[i,:] @ x) * (p1[i,:] @ x) for each head and query position.\n\nu0 · u1 = (p0 @ G) * p1 sum along last dimension  (same shape computation as norm)\n\nSo computing normm this way requires:\n- p0 @ G (B*H*L^3 FLOPs) → for cross term\n- p1 @ G (B*H*L^3 FLOPs) → for norm1\n- Elementwise operations for the combination\n\nTotal: same 2*B*H*L^3 FLOPs as current approach. No savings.\n\nBUT - what if we also compute norm0 (from p0@G) and use it for something else? Not needed.\n\nWhat about the output? Output = r * p_mix @ v = r * ((1-beta)*p0@v + beta*p1@v). We need p_mix @ v. We could compute it from p0@v and p1@v, but that's the same cost as computing p_mix then p_mix@v.\n\nHmm, let me think about this completely differently. What are all the things we need?\n\n1. out_attn = p_mix @ v  (B, H, L, D)\n2. norm1 = ||p1 @ x||  (B, H, L, 1)\n3. normm = ||p_mix @ x||  (B, H, L, 1)\n\nFor (1): we need p_mix and v. v is from projection. p_mix is from softmax blending.\nFor (2): we need p1 and G (or x).\nFor (3): we need p_mix and G (or x).\n\nWhat if we compute things in the order that minimizes memory usage / maximizes cache reuse?\n\nActually, let me revisit a thought from earlier. What if we process each head independently? Per head:\n1. q_h = x @ wq[h].T  (L × D)\n2. k_h = x @ wk[h].T  (L × D)\n3. v_h = x @ wv[h].T  (L × D)\n4. A_h = q_h @ k_h.T / sqrt(D)  (L × L)\n5. p0_h = softmax(A_h)  (L × L)\n6. p1_h = softmax(a_p*A_h + a_m*A_h.T)  (L × L)\n7. p_mix_h = lerp(p0_h, p1_h, beta)  (L × L)\n8. norm1_h = sqrt(diag(p1_h @ G @ p1_h.T))  -- via Gram\n9. normm_h = sqrt(diag(p_mix_h @ G @ p_mix_h.T))  -- via Gram\n10. out_h = (norm1_h / normm_h).clamp * (p_mix_h @ v_h)  (L × D)\n\nThen concatenate all heads.\n\nBut the projections would be H separate (L, C) @ (C, D) matmuls instead of one big (L, C) @ (C, H*D). That's worse for BLAS efficiency.\n\nUnless... we fuse the projection per head with the attention computation to keep things in cache. But the big GEMM overhead might dominate.\n\nActually, let me think about this for the v projection specifically. v_h = x @ wv[h].T is used in p_mix_h @ v_h. If we compute v_h and immediately use it, it's in cache. But p_mix_h is L×L = 256KB, v_h is L×D = 64KB, and x is L×C = 1.28MB (too big for L2).\n\nHmm, probably not a win.\n\nLet me try a completely different approach. What if we use float16 or some reduced precision for intermediate computations? No, the problem requires exact float32 output.\n\nActually wait - let me re-read the tolerance: \"abs(actual-expected) <= 3e-5 + 3e-4*abs(expected)\". That's 3e-4 relative tolerance. Float32 has ~7 decimal digits of precision, so 3e-4 is about 0.03% - much looser than float32 precision. So we have some numerical headroom.\n\nBut we can't use float16 because we can't change the compute precision... wait, actually we can use float16 internally as long as the output is float32 and meets the tolerance. But I doubt that's a net win on CPU with conversion overhead.\n\nLet me try a different angle entirely. Let me look at what `torch.compile` would do - wait, it's forbidden. What about `torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention`?\n\nLet me check if SDPA is faster than manual softmax + matmul. SDPA computes softmax(QK^T/sqrt(D)) @ V directly.\n\nWe need:\n- p0 @ v → this is SDPA(q, k, v) with scale=1/sqrt(D)\n- p1 @ v → this is SDPA with modified attention scores\n- p0 for p_mix computation\n- p1 for p_mix and norm computation\n\nWait, we need the actual attention weights (p0, p1) for the norm computation (via Gram). SDPA doesn't give us the weights. Unless we compute them separately... which defeats the purpose.\n\nActually, if we use SDPA for p_mix @ v output, but still need p1 and p_mix for norms, that doesn't save us from computing p0 and p1 explicitly.\n\nHmm. Let me think about whether there's a way to compute the norms without explicit p...\n\nActually wait - can we compute ||p@x|| from the SDPA output and something else? No, because SDPA gives us attention output in v-space, but norms are in x-space.\n\nOK let me try yet another approach. Let me see if we can optimize the softmax by using a different implementation or memory layout.\n\nActually, let me try something: instead of computing softmax on (B*H*L, L), what if we compute it on the (B, H, L, L) tensor but with a memory-contiguous last dimension? The 4D softmax on dim=-1 should already be efficient since the last dimension is contiguous.\n\nWait, is logits contiguous in its last dimension? logits = q @ k.transpose(-1, -2). q is (B, H, L, D) contiguous, k.T is (B, H, D, L). The result is (B, H, L, L) which should be contiguous in its last two dimensions. Let me verify.\n\nActually, let me just try to make sure everything is in the optimal layout and see if we can squeeze out more performance with smaller tweaks.\n\nLet me also try a key insight I just had: since we need both p0 and p1, and p0 = softmax(A), p1 = softmax(a_p*A + a_m*A^T), what if we compute the logits differently to save on the transpose?\n\nActually the transpose of logits is O(1) (just a view), it's the actual softmax that's expensive.\n\nLet me try another idea: fuse the softmax with the logits computation by doing it in-place? softmax is not in-place in PyTorch.\n\nOK, let me take stock. We're at ~47-48% of baseline. Let me think about what 10-20% improvements are still possible:\n\n1. Reshaped Gram multiply: ~2ms = ~4% improvement on largest case\n2. Removing unnecessary .contiguous() calls: maybe 1-2%\n3. Better memory layout for softmax: maybe 2-3%\n4. Fusing p_mix computation with output: not really separable\n\nLet me try implementing a version with all these small improvements and see where we land.\n\nActually, wait. I just had a big idea. Let me re-examine the operator formula:\n\nm = u0 + beta*(u1-u0)\n\nr = clamp(||u1|| / ||m||, 0.25, 4.0)\n\noutput = r * m @ wv^T\n\nWhat if we compute things in a different order that saves us from computing p_mix @ G explicitly?\n\nWe know:\n- u0 = p0 @ x\n- u1 = p1 @ x\n- m = (1-beta)*u0 + beta*u1\n\nSo:\nm @ wv^T = (1-beta) * u0 @ wv^T + beta * u1 @ wv^T = (1-beta) * (p0 @ v) + beta * (p1 @ v)\n\nAnd for norms:\n||u1|| = ||p1 @ x|| (need norm1)\n||m|| = ||(1-beta)*u0 + beta*u1|| (need normm)\n\nWe need both norms. Using Gram, both need a p@G multiply.\n\nWhat if we compute u0 and u1 directly (in x-space)? That would cost 2*B*H*L^2*C FLOPs, which for L=256, C=1280, H=20 is 2*1*20*256^2*1280 = 3.36B FLOPs, way more than the Gram approach (1.34B FLOPs).\n\nGram is better. Let me stick with it.\n\nHmm, what about a hybrid approach? Compute u1 directly (B*H*L^2*C) but use Gram for normm? Worse.\n\nOK, let me try a different strategy. Let me focus on what's achievable:\n\n1. Reshape p for Gram multiply (from 4D broadcast to 3D batched) - should be ~4% faster\n2. Try to reduce memory allocations\n3. Try torch.bmm instead of 4D @ for attention output\n4. See if we can use any other PyTorch tricks\n\nLet me implement a well-optimized version and run the full evaluation.Very interesting — reshaped Gram multiply (`(B, HL, L) @ (B, L, L)`) is ~18% faster per Gram than 4D broadcast. Let me implement a more optimized version with this and other tweaks, then run the full evaluation.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat > /tmp/profile_test5.py << 'PYEOF'\nimport time, math\nimport torch\n\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\ntorch.set_num_interop_threads(1)\n\ncases = [\n    dict(batch=1, length=64, channels=512, heads=8, dim=64, alpha=1.05, beta=5.0),\n    dict(batch=1, length=128, channels=640, heads=10, dim=64, alpha=1.1, beta=4.0),\n    dict(batch=1, length=128, channels=1280, heads=20, dim=64, alpha=1.15, beta=3.0),\n    dict(batch=1, length=256, channels=1280, heads=20, dim=64, alpha=1.05, beta=7.5),\n    dict(batch=1, length=256, channels=640, heads=10, dim=64, alpha=1.2, beta=5.0),\n    dict(batch=2, length=128, channels=512, heads=8, dim=64, alpha=1.0, beta=5.0),\n]\n\ndef ref_method(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta):\n    q = torch.einsum(\\\"blc,hdc->bhld\\\", x, wq)\n    k = torch.einsum(\\\"blc,hdc->bhld\\\", x, wk)\n    logits = (q @ k.transpose(-1, -2)) / math.sqrt(wq.shape[1])\n    p0 = logits.softmax(dim=-1)\n    base = p0 @ x.unsqueeze(1)\n    if alpha == 1.0:\n        modified = base\n    else:\n        perturbed = .5 * (1 + alpha) * logits + .5 * (1 - alpha) * logits.transpose(-1, -2)\n        modified = perturbed.softmax(dim=-1) @ x.unsqueeze(1)\n    blended = base + beta * (modified - base)\n    ratio = (modified.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True).clamp_min(1e-6)\n             / blended.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True).clamp_min(1e-6)).clamp(.25, 4.)\n    y = torch.einsum(\\\"bhlc,hdc->bhld\\\", blended * ratio, wv)\n    return y.transpose(1, 2).flatten(2).contiguous()\n\n\ndef opt_v3(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta):\n    B, L, C = x.shape\n    H, D = wq.shape[0], wq.shape[1]\n    inv_sqrt_d = 1.0 / math.sqrt(D)\n    HD = H * D\n    HL = H * L\n    xr = x.reshape(B * L, C)\n\n    q = (xr @ wq.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3)\n    k = (xr @ wk.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3)\n    v = (xr @ wv.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3)\n\n    logits = q @ k.transpose(-1, -2)\n    logits.mul_(inv_sqrt_d)\n\n    p0 = logits.softmax(dim=-1)\n\n    if alpha == 1.0:\n        y = p0 @ v\n    else:\n        a_p = 0.5 * (1.0 + alpha)\n        a_m = 0.5 * (1.0 - alpha)\n        p1_logits = logits.mul(a_p)\n        p1_logits.add_(logits.transpose(-1, -2), alpha=a_m)\n        p1 = p1_logits.softmax(dim=-1)\n\n        p_mix = torch.lerp(p0, p1, beta)\n\n        G = x @ x.transpose(-1, -2)\n\n        # Reshaped Gram multiply: (B, H*L, L) @ (B, L, L) - fewer larger matmuls\n        p1G = (p1.reshape(B, HL, L) @ G).reshape(B, H, L, L)\n        pmG = (p_mix.reshape(B, HL, L) @ G).reshape(B, H, L, L)\n\n        norm1_sq = p1G.mul_(p1).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n        normm_sq = pmG.mul_(p_mix).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n        norm1 = norm1_sq.sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n        normm = normm_sq.sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n        ratio = norm1.div_(normm).clamp_(0.25, 4.0)\n\n        y = (p_mix @ v).mul_(ratio)\n\n    return y.transpose(1, 2).flatten(2)\n\n\nprint(\\\"Testing all cases for correctness and speed...\\\")\nprint(f\\\"{'Case':>4}  {'Ref(ms)':>8}  {'Opt(ms)':>8}  {'Speedup':>7}  {'MaxDiff':>10}  {'MaxRel':>8}\\\")\nprint(\\\"-\\\" * 60)\n\ntotal_ref = 0\ntotal_opt = 0\nfor ci, case in enumerate(cases):\n    B = case[\\\"batch\\\"]; L = case[\\\"length\\\"]; C = case[\\\"channels\\\"]\n    H = case[\\\"heads\\\"]; D = case[\\\"dim\\\"]\n    alpha = case[\\\"alpha\\\"]; beta = case[\\\"beta\\\"]\n    \n    torch.manual_seed(ci * 1009 + 42)\n    x = torch.randn(B, L, C)\n    wq = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    wk = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    wv = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    \n    # Correctness\n    out_ref = ref_method(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n    out_opt = opt_v3(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n    max_diff = torch.max(torch.abs(out_ref - out_opt)).item()\n    max_rel = (torch.max(torch.abs(out_ref - out_opt) / (torch.abs(out_ref) + 1e-8))).item()\n    \n    # Warmup\n    for _ in range(3):\n        ref_method(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n        opt_v3(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n    \n    # Time\n    times_ref = []\n    times_opt = []\n    for _ in range(11):\n        t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n        ref_method(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n        times_ref.append((time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 1e6)\n        \n        t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n        opt_v3(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n        times_opt.append((time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 1e6)\n    \n    med_ref = sorted(times_ref)[5]\n    med_opt = sorted(times_opt)[5]\n    speedup = med_ref / med_opt\n    total_ref += med_ref\n    total_opt += med_opt\n    \n    print(f\\\"  {ci:2}    {med_ref:8.2f}   {med_opt:8.2f}    {speedup:.3f}x   {max_diff:.2e}   {max_rel:.2e}\\\")\n\nprint(f\\\"\\\\nMean:   {total_ref/len(cases):8.2f}   {total_opt/len(cases):8.2f}    {total_ref/total_opt:.3f}x\\\")\nprint(f\\\"Relative (opt/ref): {total_opt/total_ref:.4f}\\\")\n\nPYEOF\npython3 /tmp/profile_test5.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
