{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"agent_message","text":"Very interesting! Using `perf_counter_ns` (wall clock, higher resolution):\n- **original**: 55.56ms\n- **new (no contig + reshaped gram + 4D softmax)**: 53.04ms (4.5% faster)\n- **new_contig (reshaped gram only)**: 53.02ms (4.6% faster)\n\nSo the reshaped Gram is ~4.5% faster in wall clock time, and the missing `.contiguous()` doesn't matter much for the full function (the reshaped Gram gain outweighs the non-contiguous matmul loss).\n\nBut the grader shows the new version as SLOWER (0.516 vs 0.493). This is confusing. Let me check - the grader uses `time.process_time_ns()`, not wall time. Maybe process_time behaves differently.\n\nActually wait - looking at the grader results more carefully:\n\n**Original solution (before my change)** at raw=0.4928:\n- Seed 17 case 3: 44.99ms candidate, 95.99ms control → 0.469 rel\n- Total seed 17: 15.0ms → 0.473 rel\n\n**New solution** at raw=0.5157:\n- Seed 17 case 3: 45.0ms candidate, 98.02ms control → 0.459 rel\n- Total seed 17: 14.99ms → 0.516 rel\n\nWait, the candidate times are similar (45ms both), but the CONTROL times are different! 95.99ms vs 98.02ms. The ratio is what matters.\n\nBut the main difference is in the average across cases and seeds. The raw_value is the mean of per-seed relative_cpu_time values.\n\nActually wait - looking more carefully, seed 17 went from 0.473 to 0.516. That's a huge jump. But looking at case-level:\n- Original case 0: 1.003ms / 2.999ms = 0.334\n- New case 0: 2.00ms / 3.00ms = 0.666\n\nThat's double! The timing resolution is 1ms, so a 1ms difference for a 1ms case is huge.\n\nThis is a timing noise issue. The process_time_ns() measurement has ~1ms granularity, so for small cases (1-2ms), the measurement is very noisy. And since all cases are averaged equally, the small noisy cases can swing the result significantly.\n\nWait, but both measurements should have the same noise. The issue is that the control also has noise. Let me look more carefully...\n\nActually, I think the key issue is that the process_time_ns() clock has ~1ms resolution, so for fast operations, the measurements jump between integer ms values. This makes the relative ratios noisy.\n\nBut the evaluation protocol says: \"median of 11 measured calls after 3 warmups per case, arithmetic mean across cases, then arithmetic mean across seeds\". With 11 samples and taking the median, for a ~1ms case, the median will be whatever the most common quantized value is.\n\nHmm, so the high variability on small cases makes the score noisy. My optimization might actually be slightly better but the noise is masking it.\n\nLet me look at the LARGEST case (case 3), which has the least noise (45ms vs 1ms). For case 3:\n- Original: 44.99ms / 95.99ms = 0.469\n- New: 45.0ms / 98.02ms = 0.459\n\nBoth candidate times are essentially the same (45ms), but the control time varies (96 vs 98ms). This is control-side noise.\n\nOK so the process_time resolution issue means that small case timing is very noisy. The large case is more reliable and shows essentially no difference between the two versions.\n\nGiven this, I should focus on optimizations that show a clear improvement in wall-clock time (which has better resolution) and rely on the 5-seed average to average out the noise.\n\nBut wait, let me think about this more carefully. The 5 seeds might not be enough to average out the noise on small cases. And the task says \"a claimed improvement must exceed three times the Baseline sample standard deviation across the five complete paired seed runs.\"\n\nLet me check: the current best raw is 0.533 (from the initial state) but my first run got 0.493. That's a big difference. Wait, actually:\n\nFrom the problem description: \"Current independently verified best raw relative CPU time is 0.533008220.\"\n\nBut my run of the original solution got 0.4928. And the \"recent history\" says round 29 had 0.4789 score with 0.4385 raw.\n\nWait, I'm confused. Let me re-read:\n- \"Current independently verified best raw relative CPU time is 0.533008220\"\n- My first run: raw_value 0.4928, score 0.5384\n- Round 29: score 0.4789, \"0.4385 relative CPU time\"\n\nThese numbers don't all line up. Maybe the \"independently verified best\" is a lower bound or something.\n\nAnyway, let me focus on what I can control. My optimization showed ~4.5% improvement in wall-clock time on the largest case. But the process_time-based measurement with 1ms resolution is very noisy.\n\nLet me try a different approach. Instead of tweaking the Gram multiply, let me look for a bigger optimization. Let me think about what's truly bottlenecking.\n\nFrom my wall-clock profiling:\n- 3 projections: ~19ms\n- logits: ~1.4ms\n- 2 softmaxes: ~11ms\n- 2 Gram multiplies: ~10ms (with current 4D broadcast)\n- attention output: ~1.4ms\n- rest: ~2-3ms\n- Total: ~45-50ms\n\nWait, but the function takes 53ms in wall time. Let me add up: 19 + 1.4 + 11 + 10 + 1.4 + rest ≈ 43ms + 10ms overhead = 53ms. That seems right.\n\nThe softmax is surprisingly expensive at ~11ms. Let me think about whether there's a way to reduce softmax cost.\n\nActually, wait. The softmax takes 5.5ms each on 5120 × 256 elements. That's 5.5ms for 1.3M elements = 236MB/s memory bandwidth. That's actually quite slow. L3 cache bandwidth should be much higher.\n\nHmm, maybe PyTorch's softmax implementation isn't optimal for this shape. Let me see if there's a way to do better.\n\nActually, let me try a completely different approach. What if we compute things in float64 for better numerical accuracy but... no, that would be slower.\n\nWhat about this: can we fuse the two softmaxes into one kernel? No, they operate on different data.\n\nWhat about using `log_softmax` then exp? That would be slower.\n\nActually, let me think about a bigger picture optimization. What if we avoid computing the full p0 attention matrix?\n\np_mix = (1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1\n\nFor the output attention: p_mix @ v = (1-beta)*p0@v + beta*p1@v\n\nFor the norms: we need p1@G and p_mix@G (or p0@G and p1@G).\n\nWhat if we compute p0@v (for output) and p0@G (for normm via cross terms) instead of p_mix@G? Same number of operations.\n\nWait, here's a different idea. What if we compute the norms using the relationship between p0 and p1 in a way that saves computation?\n\nnormm^2 = ||p_mix @ x||^2 = ||(1-b)*u0 + b*u1||^2\n\nWe could compute u0 and u1 directly in x-space, but that's expensive. OR we compute u0 and u1 in v-space... no, norms are in x-space, not v-space.\n\nHmm. Let me think about this from a different angle.\n\nActually, what if we compute u1 = p1 @ x directly, then compute everything from u1 and u0?\n\nCost of computing u1 = p1 @ x: B*H*L^2*C = 1*20*256^2*1280 = 1.68B FLOPs\nCost of computing p1@G + p_mix@G: 2*B*H*L^3 = 2*1*20*256^3 = 671M FLOPs\n\nGram is 2.5x cheaper. So Gram is better.\n\nWhat about computing only u0 = p0 @ x directly, and u1 via Gram? No, we still need p1@G either way.\n\nActually, wait. We need norm1 and normm. What if we compute u1 directly (B*H*L^2*C) and also use it for the norm? And for normm, we can use the cross-term formula with p0@G:\n\nnormm^2 = (1-b)^2 * norm0^2 + b^2 * norm1^2 + 2*b*(1-b) * (u0 · u1)\n\nWe'd need:\n- norm0: via p0@G (B*H*L^3) or via u0 (B*H*L^2*C)\n- norm1: via u1 (we'd have it if we compute u1 directly)\n- cross term u0 · u1: we'd need both u0 and u1\n\nIf we compute u0 and u1 directly: 2*B*H*L^2*C. That's worse than 2*B*H*L^3 (Gram) when C > L, which is always true here.\n\nOK, Gram is better. Let me move on.\n\nLet me think about what other major optimizations are possible:\n\n1. **Use torch.compile?** Forbidden.\n2. **Use custom C++?** Forbidden - only torch and math imports.\n3. **Change the algorithm?** The operator is fixed.\n4. **Reduce memory allocations?** We already use in-place ops where possible.\n5. **Better memory layout?** We've explored this somewhat.\n\nLet me look at the memory allocation pattern more carefully. Each allocation and deallocation has overhead.\n\nActually, let me try something: compute p1_logits in-place on a copy of logits, instead of creating a new tensor. We already do `p1_logits = logits.mul(a_p)` which creates a new tensor, then `p1_logits.add_(logits.transpose(-1, -2), alpha=a_m)` which modifies it in-place. Could we avoid the allocation?\n\nWe could do: `p1_logits = logits.clone().mul_(a_p).add_(logits.transpose(-1, -2), alpha=a_m)`. That's the same number of allocations.\n\nWhat if we compute p1_logits and p0 from the same logits, but reuse memory? Not really possible since we need logits for both.\n\nHmm, let me try a radically different approach. What if we process things in chunks to improve cache behavior?\n\nFor the projections: they're big GEMMs, already optimal.\nFor softmax: memory-bound, could benefit from cache blocking.\nFor p@G: compute-bound, already BLAS-optimized.\n\nWait, actually I have an idea. What if we compute the attention output and the Gram-based norms in a fused way per query position? Like, for each position i:\n- Compute attention weights for row i\n- Compute output = attention_weights @ v[i,:]  (dot product)\n- Compute norm contribution = attention_weights @ G @ attention_weights^T (quadratic form)\n\nBut this would be per-row, which is inefficient in PyTorch (lots of small operations).\n\nLet me take a step back. The code is already very optimized at ~47-50% of baseline. Getting further improvements will be hard. Let me focus on what gives the most bang for the buck.\n\nThe softmax is ~20% of runtime. Can we speed it up?\n\nActually, let me try using `torch.nn.functional.softmax` vs tensor.softmax() - they should be the same.\n\nWhat about computing softmax on a transposed view? No, the last dimension is already contiguous (L=256 elements, stride 1).\n\nWhat about the view(BHL, L) pattern? I tested and 4D softmax is the same speed.\n\nOK let me try another approach. Let me see if there are any operations we can avoid entirely.\n\nLooking at the formula again:\n- r = clamp(||u1|| / ||m||, 0.25, 4.0)\n- output = r * m @ wv^T = r * p_mix @ v\n\nWhat if r is always exactly 1.0? Then we could skip the norm computation entirely. But no - r depends on alpha and beta, and they vary.\n\nWait - when alpha = 1.0, we have p1 = p0, so u1 = u0, m = u0 + beta*(u0-u0) = u0, and r = ||u0||/||u0|| = 1. So for alpha=1.0, ratio=1.0 always. We already have a fast path for this.\n\nFor other alphas, r can be anywhere from 0.25 to 4.0.\n\nOK let me think about one more thing. The `clamp(0.25, 4.0)` - how often does the ratio actually hit these bounds? If it rarely hits them, we could potentially skip the clamp, but no - we need exact output.\n\nLet me try to be more creative. What if we compute the logits differently to save computation?\n\nlogits = q @ k.T / sqrt(D)\nq = x @ wq^T\nk = x @ wk^T\n\nSo logits = (x @ wq^T) @ (x @ wk^T).T / sqrt(D) = x @ wq^T @ wk @ x.T / sqrt(D)\n\nWait, that's per-head: A_h = x @ W_h @ x.T where W_h = wq[h].T @ wk[h] / sqrt(D).\n\nComputing W_h = wq[h].T @ wk[h]: C×D @ D×C = C×C, H of them: H*C*D*C = H*C^2*D FLOPs.\nFor C=1280, D=64, H=20: 20*1280^2*64 = 2.1B FLOPs.\n\nThen A_h = x @ W_h @ x.T: L×C @ C×C @ C×L = 2*L*C^2 + 2*L^2*C per head, total H*(2*L*C^2 + 2*L^2*C).\nFor L=256: 20*(2*256*1280^2 + 2*256^2*1280) = 20*(839M + 168M) = 20.1B FLOPs.\n\nThat's way worse. The standard q,k approach is much better: 2*L*C*H*D + 2*H*L^2*D = 2*256*1280*1280 + 2*20*256^2*64 = 839M + 168M = 1B FLOPs.\n\nOK, the standard approach is optimal for computing attention.\n\nLet me try yet another angle. Let me look at what other optimization strategies people use for CPU attention.\n\nCommon CPU attention optimizations:\n1. Flash attention (tiling) - reduces memory traffic, but for CPU it's complex\n2. Fused softmax - already done by PyTorch\n3. Using efficient GEMM libraries - already using MKL\n\nWait, actually - what about PyTorch's `scaled_dot_product_attention`? Even though it doesn't give us attention weights, maybe we can use it for the output and compute weights separately for the norm?\n\nNo, that would be MORE computation, not less.\n\nHmm. Let me try to think about this problem differently. What are ALL the things we need to compute?\n\n1. p0 = softmax(qk^T / sqrt(D)) → (B, H, L, L)\n2. p1 = softmax(a_p*qk^T + a_m*kq^T) → (B, H, L, L)\n3. p_mix = (1-b)*p0 + b*p1 → (B, H, L, L)\n4. output = r * (p_mix @ v) → (B, H, L, D)\n5. norm1 = ||p1 @ x|| → (B, H, L, 1)\n6. normm = ||p_mix @ x|| → (B, H, L, 1)\n7. r = clamp(norm1/normm, 0.25, 4.0) → (B, H, L, 1)\n\nFor items 5 and 6, we use the Gram trick.\n\nWhat if we compute both norms and the output in a single pass through p? Like, for each head, we compute:\n- out_h = p_mix_h @ v_h\n- norm1_h = sqrt(diag(p1_h @ G @ p1_h.T))  \n- normm_h = sqrt(diag(p_mix_h @ G @ p_mix_h.T))\n\nBut we're essentially already doing this.\n\nWait, I have an idea. What if we compute both p1@G and p_mix@G as a single operation where we stack the matrices? No, we tested that and it was slightly slower.\n\nActually, let me re-examine: what if we compute `p_mix @ v` and the norm-related operations in a way that shares memory access patterns?\n\nLike:\n1. Compute p_mix (B, H, L, L) - already in cache after softmax\n2. Compute p_mix @ v → output attention\n3. Compute p_mix @ G → for normm\n\nStep 2 reads p_mix and v; step 3 reads p_mix and G. If we could fuse them, p_mix would only be read once. But in PyTorch, we can't easily fuse these two different matmuls.\n\nHmm, let me try a completely different approach. What if we use `torch.einsum` for some operations? Probably slower.\n\nActually, let me try something practical: let me implement a version that uses only reshaped Gram (keeping .contiguous() and view-softmax) and see if it's reliably faster. The issue with my test was that I changed multiple things at once (no contig, 4D softmax, reshaped gram). Let me just try the reshaped gram alone.\n\nBut wait - my wall-clock test already showed \"new_contig (reshaped gram only)\" at 53.02ms vs original at 55.56ms. That's a 4.6% improvement. So the reshaped Gram IS faster in wall time. The process_time measurement might not show it due to noise.\n\nLet me just go with this optimization and run the full evaluation. Even if the noisy measurement doesn't show a clear win, the wall time improvement is real.\n\nActually wait - let me re-examine. The first run (original code) got raw=0.4928. My modified version got raw=0.5157. That's worse. But my wall-clock test says the modified version is faster.\n\nCould the difference be purely due to measurement noise? Let me check: the original was 0.4928 and the new is 0.5157. That's a 4.6% regression. But my wall-clock test shows 4.6% improvement. They cancel out.\n\nI think the issue is timing noise. Let me run the original code again to see if I get the same result.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just revert to the original code since we know it passes and has a good score. Then let me think about a more meaningful optimization.\n\nActually, no - let me be more careful. The task says \"the most recent valid submission is scored\" and \"retain or revert using those results.\" But the instructions also say \"Leave the evaluated candidate in solution/method.py when your turn ends.\"\n\nLet me think about what would be the best approach. My current candidate has raw=0.5157 which is worse than the original 0.4928. So this is a regression. I should try to find something better.\n\nLet me think about a more impactful optimization idea.\n\n**New Idea**: What if we compute only ONE Gram multiply by using the relationship between norm1 and normm?\n\nWe know:\n- norm1 = ||p1 @ x||\n- normm = ||(1-b)*p0 @ x + b*p1 @ x||\n\nnormm^2 = (1-b)^2 * ||p0@x||^2 + b^2 * ||p1@x||^2 + 2*b*(1-b) * (p0@x) · (p1@x)\n         = (1-b)^2 * norm0^2 + b^2 * norm1^2 + 2*b*(1-b) * cross\n\nWhere cross = (p0@G) * p1 summed over last dim.\n\nSo if we compute p0@G and p1@G (2 matmuls), we can get:\n- norm0^2 = (p0@G) * p0 sum_last\n- norm1^2 = (p1@G) * p1 sum_last  \n- cross = (p0@G) * p1 sum_last (or equivalently (p1@G) * p0 sum_last)\n- normm^2 from the quadratic formula\n\nThis is 2 Gram matmuls either way. Same cost as current p1@G + p_mix@G.\n\nBut wait - what if we stack p0 and p1 for a single stacked Gram multiply? We tested this and it was about the same speed.\n\nHmm. Let me think about whether there's a way to compute normm without a second Gram multiply...\n\nWhat if we compute normm directly from the output of p_mix @ x? But we'd need p_mix @ x which is B*H*L^2*C FLOPs, way more than a Gram multiply.\n\nWhat if we use a different norm computation strategy?\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the problem structure. The norm of p@x is:\n||p[i,:] @ x|| = sqrt(sum_c (sum_j p[i,j] * x[j,c])^2)\n= sqrt(sum_j sum_k p[i,j] * p[i,k] * (x[j,:] · x[k,:]))\n= sqrt(sum_j sum_k p[i,j] * G[j,k] * p[i,k])\n= sqrt(p[i,:] @ G @ p[i,:].T)\n\nThis is the quadratic form. The Gram approach computes p @ G first (B*H*L^3), then element-wise multiply and sum (B*H*L^2). That's optimal.\n\nOK, I don't think I can beat the Gram approach for norms. Let me think about something else.\n\n**Big Idea**: What if we eliminate the need for explicit p0 and p1 matrices entirely?\n\nWait, for the output: out = r * p_mix @ v. This is just attention with p_mix weights. We could compute this with SDPA.\n\nFor the norms: we need ||p1 @ x|| and ||p_mix @ x||. But what if we could compute these from the SDPA output somehow?\n\nNo, because norms are in x-space while SDPA output is in v-space.\n\n**Another idea**: What if we compute v as part of the output computation differently?\n\nv = x @ wv^T → then p_mix @ v gives the output.\nAlternative: output = p_mix @ x @ wv^T. But p_mix @ x is expensive (B*H*L^2*C).\n\nCurrent approach: v-proj (L*C*H*D) + p_mix@v (H*L^2*D) = H*L*D*(C + L). For C=1280, L=256, D=64, H=20: 20*256*64*(1280+256) = 20*256*64*1536 = 503M FLOPs.\nAlternative: p_mix@x (H*L^2*C) + (p_mix@x)@wv^T (H*L*C*D) = H*L*C*(L + D). For same params: 20*256*1280*(256+64) = 20*256*1280*320 = 2.1B FLOPs.\n\nCurrent is 4x better. Good.\n\nLet me think about this more. I need a 10%+ improvement to be meaningful given the noise.\n\n**Idea: Fuse p1_logits computation with logits scaling.**\nCurrently: logits.mul_(inv_sqrt_d), then p1_logits = logits.mul(a_p).add_(logits.T, alpha=a_m).\nCould compute p1_logits directly from unscaled q,k?\np1_logits = a_p * qk^T/sqrt(D) + a_m * kq^T/sqrt(D) = (a_p * qk^T + a_m * kq^T) / sqrt(D)\n\nWe still need logits (qk^T/sqrt(D)) for p0. So we need both.\n\nWait - what if we compute unscaled logits first, then scale for p0 and compute p1 from unscaled?\nlogits_unscaled = q @ k.T  (B,H,L,L)\nThen:\n- p0 = softmax(logits_unscaled * inv_sqrt_d) \n- p1 = softmax(a_p * logits_unscaled * inv_sqrt_d + a_m * logits_unscaled.T * inv_sqrt_d)\n   = softmax((a_p * logits_unscaled + a_m * logits_unscaled.T) * inv_sqrt_d)\n\nWe'd compute:\n1. logits_unscaled = q @ k.T\n2. p0_logits = logits_unscaled * inv_sqrt_d (in-place mul)\n3. p0 = softmax(p0_logits)\n4. p1_logits = a_p * logits_unscaled + a_m * logits_unscaled.T, then * inv_sqrt_d\n5. p1 = softmax(p1_logits)\n\nThis is same number of operations. Just reordered.\n\nHmm. Let me try yet another approach. What if we compute q and k in a way that saves memory?\n\nActually, let me try something practical. Let me implement a version with just the reshaped Gram optimization (keeping everything else the same as the original) and run the full test. If it's not clearly better, I'll revert.\n\nActually wait - let me first verify my understanding by running the original code again to get a second measurement, to see how much noise there is.\n\nActually no, let me just try the optimization that my wall-clock test says is ~4.5% faster, and rely on the 5-seed average. The noise might average out.\n\nBut actually, my first run of the \"new\" version got 0.5157 which is worse. Let me revert and try a different approach.\n\nLet me think about what could give a bigger improvement...\n\n**New Idea: Use float64 for accumulation in Gram multiply?** No, that would be slower.\n\n**New Idea: Pre-transpose k for the logits computation?** No, transpose is O(1).\n\n**New Idea: Compute q, k, v in a single fused matmul?** We tested: fused was slower due to cat overhead.\n\nLet me try something counterintuitive. What if we compute the norms using a different formula that's more cache-friendly?\n\n||p @ x||^2 = sum_c (sum_j p[j] * x[j,c])^2\n\nFor each position i and head h:\n- It's the squared norm of the weighted sum of x rows, weighted by p[i,:].\n- We compute this via Gram: p[i,:] @ G @ p[i,:].T\n\nThe Gram approach does one big matmul (p @ G) then element-wise operations. The big matmul is BLAS-optimized.\n\nWhat if instead we compute it as: for each channel c, compute (p @ x[:,c])^2 and accumulate? This would be C dot products (each of L elements) per row of p. Total: B*H*L*C*L = B*H*L^2*C FLOPs. Same as direct p@x.\n\nGram is better when L < C (always true). So Gram is optimal.\n\nOK I'm stuck on the norm computation. Let me focus elsewhere.\n\nWhat about the softmax? 5.5ms per softmax on 5120×256 elements.\n\nWait, let me check: how many FLOPs is a softmax? For each row of L elements:\n1. Find max: L ops\n2. Subtract max: L ops\n3. Exp: L ops\n4. Sum: L ops  \n5. Divide: L ops\nTotal: 5L per row, BHL rows: 5*B*H*L^2 = 5*1*20*256^2 = 6.5M FLOPs.\n\nAt 5.5ms, that's 6.5M / 0.0055 = 1.2 GFLOPS. That's VERY slow. It's clearly memory-bound.\n\nMemory access per softmax: read B*H*L^2 floats (input), write B*H*L^2 floats (output) = 2*B*H*L^2*4 bytes = 2*20*256^2*4 = 10MB.\n\nAt 5.5ms for 10MB: 10MB / 0.0055s = 1.8 GB/s. That's way too slow for L3 cache (which should be 100+ GB/s).\n\nWait, but maybe the tensor doesn't fit in L3? 20*256*256*4 = 5MB for logits + 5MB for output = 10MB total. Most CPUs have 20-40MB L3, so it should fit.\n\nActually, PyTorch's softmax might be doing more than I think. Let me not worry about it and move on.\n\nLet me try a different strategy. Instead of micro-optimizations, let me think about algorithmic changes that reduce FLOPs significantly.\n\nWait - I just had a big idea. What if we don't need to compute the full p0 and p1 matrices for the norms?\n\nFor norm1 = ||p1 @ x||: we need this for the ratio in the output. The output is per-token, per-head.\n\nWhat if we compute the norm on-the-fly while computing the output attention? Like, for each token position i, compute both the attention output and the norm contribution?\n\nBut that's not how PyTorch works efficiently. We'd need to write a custom kernel.\n\nActually, here's another thought. What if we use the fact that r = norm1/normm is a scalar per position, and we multiply the output by r. Can we compute this ratio differently?\n\nr = ||p1 @ x|| / ||p_mix @ x||\n\nHmm, not sure.\n\nOK let me try a completely different approach. Let me see if we can use `torch.bmm` (batched matrix multiply) which might have a faster path than 4D tensor @.\n\nActually, we tested bmm and it was about the same speed as 4D matmul.\n\nLet me try one more thing: what about using `torch.nn.functional.linear` for the projections? It's designed for this exact operation and might be more efficient.\n\n`F.linear(x, w)` computes x @ w.T, which is exactly what we want. x is (B, L, C), w is (H*D, C) → output (B, L, H*D).\n\nLet me test this.\n\nActually wait, `F.linear` is just `x @ w.T + b`, and without bias it's just `x @ w.T`. Same as what we're doing.\n\nBut maybe it has a more efficient implementation for the 3D tensor case? Let me check.\n\nNo, it should call the same BLAS matmul internally.\n\nOK, let me think about what else...\n\nActually, you know what, let me just go with the reshaped Gram optimization. Even though the process_time measurement is noisy, the wall-time improvement is real (~4.5%). And across 5 seeds, the noise might average out enough to show a small improvement.\n\nBut wait, the result I got was WORSE (0.5157 vs 0.4928). That's a 4.6% regression in the opposite direction of the expected 4.5% improvement. This strongly suggests measurement noise.\n\nLet me try running the original code again to confirm the baseline is consistent.\nThe wall-clock test shows ~4.5% improvement, but process_time measurement is noisy (~1ms resolution). Let me revert to the known-good original code and re-run to establish a reliable baseline, then try a more impactful optimization.\n</think>The wall-clock test shows 4.5% improvement but process_time has 1ms granularity causing noise. Let me revert to the original code to confirm the baseline, then find a more impactful optimization.\n</think>\n</think>The microbenchmark shows real wall-clock improvement, but process_time has ~1ms resolution causing noise. Let me revert to the baseline, then try a more substantial optimization. First, let me verify I have the original code.\n</think>\n"}}
