{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_55","type":"agent_message","text":"I have the current modified version. Let me restore the original optimized version from my first read. It was:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Optimized multihead retrieval kernel: v-space projections, 2D softmax, Gram norms, 4D p@G.\"\"\"\nimport math\nimport torch\n\n\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef attention(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta):\n    B, L, C = x.shape\n    H, D = wq.shape[0], wq.shape[1]\n    inv_sqrt_d = 1.0 / math.sqrt(D)\n    HD = H * D\n    BHL = B * H * L\n    xr = x.reshape(B * L, C)\n\n    q = (xr @ wq.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\n    k = (xr @ wk.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\n    v = (xr @ wv.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\n\n    logits = q @ k.transpose(-1, -2)\n    logits.mul_(inv_sqrt_d)\n\n    p0 = logits.view(BHL, L).softmax(dim=-1).view(B, H, L, L)\n\n    if alpha == 1.0:\n        y = p0 @ v\n    else:\n        a_p = 0.5 * (1.0 + alpha)\n        a_m = 0.5 * (1.0 - alpha)\n        p1_logits = logits.mul(a_p)\n        p1_logits.add_(logits.transpose(-1, -2), alpha=a_m)\n        p1 = p1_logits.view(BHL, L).softmax(dim=-1).view(B, H, L, L)\n\n        p_mix = torch.lerp(p0, p1, beta)\n\n        G = x @ x.transpose(-1, -2)\n        G_exp = G.unsqueeze(1)\n\n        p1G = p1 @ G_exp\n        norm1_sq = p1G.mul_(p1).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n        pmG = p_mix @ G_exp\n        normm_sq = pmG.mul_(p_mix).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n        norm1 = norm1_sq.sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n        normm = normm_sq.sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n        ratio = norm1.div_(normm).clamp_(0.25, 4.0)\n\n        y = (p_mix @ v).mul_(ratio)\n\n    return y.transpose(1, 2).flatten(2).contiguous()\n```\n\nLet me restore this and then think about a more impactful optimization.\n\nActually, wait. Before I restore, let me think about what would be a really impactful optimization.\n\n**Big Idea: Reduce from 2 Gram multiplies to 1.**\n\nWe need norm1 and normm. Currently we compute p1@G and p_mix@G.\n\nWhat if we compute normm using norm1 and a cross term?\nnormm^2 = (1-b)^2 * norm0^2 + b^2 * norm1^2 + 2*b*(1-b) * cross\n\nWhere cross = (p0 @ G) * p1 summed over last dim.\n\nBut this still requires p0@G, which is another Gram multiply. Same total.\n\nUNLESS... we can find a way to express the ratio r = norm1/normm without computing both individually?\n\nr = norm1 / normm = ||p1@x|| / ||p_mix@x||\nr^2 = ||p1@x||^2 / ||p_mix@x||^2\n\nCan we compute this ratio differently?\n\nr^2 = [sum_j sum_k p1[i,j] * G[j,k] * p1[i,k]] / [sum_j sum_k p_mix[i,j] * G[j,k] * p_mix[i,k]]\n\nNot obviously simplifiable.\n\n**Alternative Big Idea: Can we avoid computing p0 softmax entirely?**\n\np_mix = (1-b)*p0 + b*p1\np0 = softmax(A)\np1 = softmax(a_p*A + a_m*A^T)\n\nThese are different softmaxes of different inputs. No way to derive one from the other.\n\n**Another Idea: Can we use attention output to help compute norms?**\n\noutput = p_mix @ v = p_mix @ x @ wv^T\nnorm = ||p_mix @ x||\n\nIf wv were orthogonal, then norm of output would be related to norm of p_mix@x. But wv is generally not orthogonal.\n\nWhat if we compute ||p_mix @ x||^2 = trace((p_mix @ x) @ (p_mix @ x).T) ... no, we need per-row norms.\n\nPer row i: ||p_mix[i,:] @ x||^2 = p_mix[i,:] @ G @ p_mix[i,:].T\n\nThat's the quadratic form, which is what Gram computes. There's no shortcut.\n\n**What about using the Woodbury formula or some other matrix identity?**\n\nG = x @ x.T is rank min(L,C) = L (since L < C). G is L×L, symmetric, positive semi-definite.\n\nThe quadratic form p @ G @ p.T per row... there's no faster way to compute all rows than computing p @ G first (which gives us the L-vector for each row), then dotting with p.\n\nOK, I think the Gram approach is optimal for norms.\n\n**What about fusing the output computation with the norm computation?**\n\nBoth p_mix @ v and p_mix @ G involve p_mix. If we could compute them in a single pass where p_mix is read only once, we'd save memory bandwidth.\n\np_mix is B*H*L^2 floats. For the largest case: 20*256^2*4 = 5.2MB. Reading it twice is 10.4MB of memory traffic. At 10 GB/s, that's ~1ms. Not huge, but measurable.\n\nBut in PyTorch, we can't easily fuse two matmuls.\n\nHmm, let me try a completely different angle. What if we change the order of operations to improve cache reuse?\n\nCurrent order:\n1. Projections (q, k, v) - read x, wq, wk, wv\n2. Logits (q @ k.T) - read q, k\n3. p0 softmax - read/write logits\n4. p1 logits + softmax - read logits\n5. p_mix = lerp(p0, p1) - read p0, p1\n6. G = x @ x.T - read x\n7. p1 @ G - read p1, G\n8. p_mix @ G - read p_mix, G\n9. Norms + ratio\n10. p_mix @ v - read p_mix, v\n11. Output = y * ratio\n\nWhat if we compute G earlier (step 6 before step 3), so that G is computed while x is still in cache from the projections?\n\nx is B*L*C floats. For largest case: 256*1280*4 = 1.3MB. That fits in L2. After the projections, x should still be in L2 cache. Computing G = x@x.T immediately after would reuse x in cache.\n\nThen later when we need p@G, G is already computed and possibly still in cache.\n\nLet me test this reordering.\n\nActually, another thought. What if we compute the projections and G in an order that maximizes cache reuse?\n\nFor the v projection: v = x @ wv^T. After computing q and k, x might be evicted from cache. Computing G right after q projection would reuse x while it's still hot.\n\nWait, we compute q, k, v projections sequentially. After q and k projections, x has been read twice. After v projection, three times. x is 1.3MB, which fits in L2 (typically 256KB-1MB per core, but L3 is much larger). So x stays in L3.\n\nHmm, this is getting into micro-optimization territory. Let me think about bigger wins.\n\n**Actually, here's a big one I missed.** The output is `(r*m) @ wv^T`, where m = u0 + beta*(u1-u0) = p_mix @ x.\n\nWe compute this as `(p_mix @ v) * ratio` where v = x @ wv^T. That's correct.\n\nBut what if we compute the output differently? What if we compute it as:\noutput = r * (p_mix @ x) @ wv^T\n\nInstead of:\nv = x @ wv^T\noutput = r * (p_mix @ v)\n\nThese are mathematically equivalent (associativity of matrix multiplication). But the computational cost is different:\n- Current: H*D*L*C (v-proj) + H*L^2*D (p_mix@v) = H*L*D*(C + L)\n- Alternative: H*L^2*C (p_mix@x) + H*L*C*D (output proj) = H*L*C*(L + D)\n\nFor C >> D (which is the case: C=1280, D=64), current approach is better because:\n- Current: H*L*D*C + H*L^2*D ≈ H*L*D*C (since C >> L)\n- Alternative: H*L*C*L + H*L*C*D ≈ H*L^2*C (since L >> D)\n\nWait no, let me be more precise:\n- Current cost: 2*H*L*C*D + 2*H*L^2*D = 2*H*L*D*(C + L)\n  = 2*20*256*64*(1280+256) = 2*20*256*64*1536 = 1.01B FLOPs\n\n- Alternative: 2*H*L^2*C + 2*H*L*C*D = 2*H*L*C*(L + D)\n  = 2*20*256*1280*(256+64) = 2*20*256*1280*320 = 4.19B FLOPs\n\nCurrent is 4x better. Good.\n\nBut wait - what if we DON'T need to compute all three projections (q, k, v) separately? q and k are both x @ w^T. They could be computed together. But they use different weights (wq and wk), so they need separate matmuls. However, x is shared. So computing them as one big matmul with [wq; wk] (stacked weights) would read x only once.\n\nLet me test this idea! Stack wq and wk into one big weight matrix, compute q and k together, then split.\n\nx @ [wq^T, wk^T] = [q, k] (concatenated along the last dim).\n\nThis replaces 2 matmuls of (B*L, C) @ (C, H*D) with 1 matmul of (B*L, C) @ (C, 2*H*D). Same FLOPs but better cache reuse of x.\n\nSimilarly with all three: stack wq, wk, wv and do one big matmul.\n\nWe tested this earlier and it was slightly slower (19.38 vs 21.12ms). But wait, that was including the reshape and split. Let me re-test more carefully.\n\nActually wait, looking at my earlier test:\n- 3 separate projs: 19.38 ms\n- fused proj + split: 21.12 ms\n\nThe fused version was SLOWER. That might be because the split + resh